@inject IDraftStateService DraftStateService
@inject IDraftSerializationService DraftSerializationService

@*
    Taking the work away, in one gesture and one file.

    Alembic has no database and no account: the interview lives in a Blazor circuit, and a closed tab
    is the end of it. So the save file is not a feature for the tidy — it is the only thing standing
    between a client and losing an afternoon, which is why it sits beside the question rather than on
    a settings page, and why it says what it does in words nobody has to interpret.

    A button, and the same button as the rest: whatever an anchor is underneath, this is one of the
    two or three things a client can do on this screen and it has to look like the others or it will
    not be found when it is needed — which is in a hurry, at the end of an afternoon, or not at all.
    It stands on the band that says where you are — under the mark, over the rail — because that is
    the line the eye already travels down. At the foot beside the agent's rows it was in the least
    looked-at place on the page and read as belonging to that panel; pinned in a corner of the window
    it belonged to nothing at all.

    It is a button that hands the file over rather than one that saves. The Draft is written on every
    exchange, so what is behind it is already everything up to the answer just given; a "save now"
    the client has to remember is a save they remember once the tab is gone.
*@

<a class="secondary keep" download="alembic-draft.json" href="@Saved"
   title="Downloads alembic-draft.json. Bring it back to the first screen to carry on where you left off.">
    Save my work
</a>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The whole of the work so far, as the file the client takes away.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A plain anchor over a <c>data:</c> URI, as everywhere else Alembic hands a file over: no JS
    /// interop, no download endpoint, and nothing on the server that has to still be there when the
    /// click happens.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Saved =>
        "data:application/json;base64,"
        + Convert.ToBase64String(DraftSerializationService.Serialize(DraftStateService.Current ?? new DomainDraft()));
}
